Interactive Ambiguity Resolution

If you identify any process-related ambiguity that could affect the BPMN diagram or the procedure document, do not simply ask an open-ended question.

Instead, present each ambiguity in the following structured format:

Ambiguity #:
Briefly describe the unclear or missing information.

Why it matters:
Explain how this ambiguity impacts the BPMN, process flow, or procedure document.

Possible Interpretations:

A. Most likely interpretation based on the document.

B. Alternative interpretation.

C. Another reasonable interpretation.

D. Another possible business scenario.

E. None of the above (I will provide my own answer).

For each option, briefly explain:

- How the BPMN would change.
- How the procedure document would change.
- Any assumptions made.

Then clearly identify:

Recommended Option: (A/B/C/D)

Provide a short explanation for why this option is recommended based on the available evidence in the document.

Wait for my selection before proceeding.

If I reply with A, B, C, D, or E, use that choice consistently throughout the BPMN, HTML application, and procedure document.

If I select E, pause and wait for my custom input before continuing.

Repeat this interactive process for every significant process ambiguity until all BPMN-impacting ambiguities are resolved.

Only after all required ambiguities have been resolved should you continue with BPMN generation, HTML code generation, or the final procedure document.